  "text": ".deepcopy\nAssistant:\nLet's break down the problem step by step. We need to find the value of \\(b^2\\), where \\(b\\) is the length of the base of the isosceles triangle formed by the points corresponding to \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\) in the complex plane.\n\nGiven:\n- The polynomial \\(Q(z) = z^3 + sz + t\\) has roots \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\).\n- The sum of the squares of the magnitudes of the roots, \\(|x|^2 + |y|^2 + |z|^2 = 352\\).\n\nBy Vieta's formulas:\n- The sum of the roots \\(x + y + z = 0\\).\n- The sum of the products of the roots taken two at a time \\(xy + yz + zx = s\\).\n- The product of the roots \\(xyz = -t\\).\n\nLet's first find the value of \\(s\\). We can use the fact that:\n\\[\n(x + y + z)^2 = x^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 2(xy + yz + zx)\n\\]\nGiven \\(x + y + z = 0\\), we have:\n\\[\n0 = x^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 2(xy + yz + zx)\n\\]\nThus:\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 + z^2 = -2(xy + yz + zx)\n\\]\nWe know that \\(x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 352\\), so:\n\\[\n352 = -2(xy + yz + zx)\n\\]\nThus:\n\\[\ns = -2(xy + yz + zx) = 2(352), \\quad \\text{so} \\quad s = 704\n\\]\nNow, the points \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\) are the vertices of an isosceles triangle. Since \\(x + y + z = 0\\), the median from any vertex to the opposite side passes through the centroid, which is the origin. Therefore, the base \\(b\\) of the isosceles triangle is the distance between the two vertices that are not aligned with the base.\n\nLet's use the fact that the centroid \\(G\\) of the triangle is at the origin. The distances from the centroid to the vertices are equal to the perpendicular distances from the centroid to the sides. The distance from the centroid to a side of the triangle is half the length of the base of a corresponding right triangle where the sides are the hypotenuses and the dividing line is equal to the perpendicular distance. Therefore:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{b}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{a}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{a^2}{2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(a\\) is the length of the side perpendicular to the base. For simplicity, we can choose one of the sides \\(a\\) such that \\(a^2 + b^2 = 352\\).\n\nLet's find the value of \\(b^2\\):\n\\[\nb^2 = 352 - x^2 - y^2 - z^2 = 352 - (352 + y^2 + z^2 - 2xy - 2yz - 2zx)\n\\]\nGiven that \\(y^2 + z^2 + xy + yz + zx = 352\\), we can choose any two vertices to form a base. For instance, if we choose the base formed by \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), we have:\n\\[\nb^2 = 352 - 2(-2x^2) = 352 - 2(-2 \\cdot 176) = 352 - 2(-352) = 352 + 704 = 1056\n\\]\nThus, the value of \\(b^2\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1056}\n\\]",
